1
00:00:01,551 --> 00:00:03,780
Previously
on Stargate SG-1.

2
00:00:03,804 --> 00:00:07,492
I'm not just interested
in outer space,

3
00:00:07,516 --> 00:00:09,494
I'm from outer space.

4
00:00:09,518 --> 00:00:11,037
Four excellent heroes

5
00:00:11,061 --> 00:00:14,081
in an extraordinary
new sci-fi series,

6
00:00:14,105 --> 00:00:17,668
starring Nick Marlowe
as the wry Colonel Danning.

7
00:00:17,692 --> 00:00:21,672
As a matter of fact, it does
say "Colonel" on my uniform.

8
00:00:21,696 --> 00:00:24,675
Wormhole X-treme.

9
00:00:24,699 --> 00:00:26,552
It's what I do.

10
00:00:26,576 --> 00:00:28,221
He sold
the idea to the studio.

11
00:00:28,245 --> 00:00:29,680
He obviously got
the whole thing

12
00:00:29,704 --> 00:00:31,098
from his knowledge of
the Stargate Program.

13
00:00:31,122 --> 00:00:32,809
No, if the Air Force
didn't want it to happen,

14
00:00:32,833 --> 00:00:35,001
they wouldn't have
let it go this far.

15
00:00:37,712 --> 00:00:40,525
Come on out.

16
00:00:40,549 --> 00:00:43,361
We are the Furlings.

17
00:00:43,385 --> 00:00:46,012
We thought we'd
never get to meet you.

18
00:00:52,602 --> 00:00:55,313
We led them
straight here.

19
00:00:58,024 --> 00:01:00,044
Sam! I thought I
could make it work.

20
00:01:00,068 --> 00:01:04,948
The thermodynamic loop is
feeding back into the planet's core.

21
00:01:12,622 --> 00:01:13,724
Well, that never happened.

22
00:01:13,748 --> 00:01:15,893
Well, you've got to open big,
catch people's attention,

23
00:01:15,917 --> 00:01:19,689
make them think the whole
thing is going to be jam-packed.

24
00:01:19,713 --> 00:01:21,047
I love jam.

25
00:01:22,632 --> 00:01:23,192
Oh, I get it,

26
00:01:23,216 --> 00:01:27,655
it's yet another playful twist on
words in your Earth language.

27
00:01:27,679 --> 00:01:29,824
It makes us look stupid.

28
00:01:29,848 --> 00:01:31,742
You realize
it's not actually us.

29
00:01:31,766 --> 00:01:34,328
Okay, okay, it makes
the characters look stupid.

30
00:01:34,352 --> 00:01:37,957
I mean, do you really think the best
way to introduce the heroes of the story

31
00:01:37,981 --> 00:01:40,668
is to show them causing
a massive catastrophe?

32
00:01:40,692 --> 00:01:42,753
That's realistic.

33
00:01:42,777 --> 00:01:43,754
I don't care.

34
00:01:43,778 --> 00:01:44,755
Come on. It'll be fun.

35
00:01:44,779 --> 00:01:46,424
You know, you say
that about everything.

36
00:01:46,448 --> 00:01:48,175
Come to think of it,
why am I doing this?

37
00:01:48,199 --> 00:01:51,888
To make sure that the Air Force
is being properly represented

38
00:01:51,912 --> 00:01:54,348
and because General O'Neill

39
00:01:54,372 --> 00:01:57,435
has requested you
specifically.

40
00:01:57,459 --> 00:01:58,769
Of course he did.

41
00:01:58,793 --> 00:02:00,771
Jackson, how often
do we get to give

42
00:02:00,795 --> 00:02:02,773
notes on a big Hollywood
feature script?

43
00:02:02,797 --> 00:02:04,775
I believe this is intended
to be a television movie.

44
00:02:04,799 --> 00:02:10,072
A fictionalized, albeit slightly
ridiculous, version of Stargate Command

45
00:02:10,096 --> 00:02:12,033
is an excellent cover
for the real thing

46
00:02:12,057 --> 00:02:14,035
in the event of
a security leak.

47
00:02:14,059 --> 00:02:15,202
Plausible deniability.

48
00:02:15,226 --> 00:02:17,163
I'm sorry, sir, did you
say slightly ridiculous?

49
00:02:17,187 --> 00:02:20,041
You're doing this.
You have no choice.

50
00:02:20,065 --> 00:02:21,876
Just give the man
five minutes.

51
00:02:21,900 --> 00:02:24,903
I'll get you out of this
when the time comes.

52
00:02:26,780 --> 00:02:29,258
Um, I've been
looking through this,

53
00:02:29,282 --> 00:02:31,510
and there doesn't seem
to be any mention of

54
00:02:31,534 --> 00:02:36,140
a sexy female alien anywhere.

55
00:02:36,164 --> 00:02:37,183
I'm not even in this.

56
00:02:37,207 --> 00:02:39,268
You know,
the really unbelievable part

57
00:02:39,292 --> 00:02:40,770
is that anyone would
consider spending

58
00:02:40,794 --> 00:02:42,813
millions of dollars
this thing.

59
00:02:42,837 --> 00:02:44,315
I mean, seriously,
all these writers

60
00:02:44,339 --> 00:02:45,816
and they couldn't come up
with anything better?

61
00:02:45,840 --> 00:02:48,819
I don't know, I've been
watching a lot of television lately

62
00:02:48,843 --> 00:02:50,821
and, apart from
one glaring omission,

63
00:02:50,845 --> 00:02:52,823
it doesn't seem
to be that bad.

64
00:02:52,847 --> 00:02:55,826
Maybe we can make it better.

65
00:02:55,850 --> 00:02:56,494
No, I'm with Sam on this one.

66
00:02:56,518 --> 00:02:58,162
I mean, who makes
a movie out of a series

67
00:02:58,186 --> 00:02:59,747
that only lasted
three episodes?

68
00:02:59,771 --> 00:03:02,833
It allegedly
performed well on DVD.

69
00:03:02,857 --> 00:03:06,462
No, I'm not using any shots
from the series.

70
00:03:06,486 --> 00:03:09,548
It's a movie,
not a clip show.

71
00:03:09,572 --> 00:03:10,466
The budget
is the budget.

72
00:03:10,490 --> 00:03:14,553
They are a bunch of
pencil-pushing, bean-counting idiots.

73
00:03:14,577 --> 00:03:16,847
You tell them
if they don't like it,

74
00:03:16,871 --> 00:03:19,749
I'm going to take it
somewhere else.

75
00:03:21,751 --> 00:03:25,171
You know not to say
that last part, right?

76
00:03:26,214 --> 00:03:29,819
Yeah, movie,
not clip show, is fine.

77
00:03:29,843 --> 00:03:34,740
Yeah, I've got to go. Yeah.

78
00:03:34,764 --> 00:03:39,203
Your cell coverage is
just terrible in here.

79
00:03:39,227 --> 00:03:41,479
How's it going? You done?

80
00:03:44,983 --> 00:03:50,381
Well, it certainly seems
to be packed full of jam.

81
00:03:50,405 --> 00:03:52,425
I knew I should have
given you my first draft.

82
00:03:52,449 --> 00:03:55,886
The producer brought
in the other writers.

83
00:03:55,910 --> 00:03:57,555
I thought
you were the producer.

84
00:03:57,579 --> 00:03:59,140
Yeah, the real one.

85
00:03:59,164 --> 00:04:01,392
Look, just be honest.

86
00:04:01,416 --> 00:04:04,854
I trust you guys.
That's why I'm here.

87
00:04:04,878 --> 00:04:07,505
Just tell me
what you think.

88
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:13,029
Come on,
you must have some ideas.

89
00:04:13,053 --> 00:04:15,906
Don't be afraid.
Just pitch them out.

90
00:04:15,930 --> 00:04:17,908
We call it spinning.

91
00:04:17,932 --> 00:04:21,495
Don't worry.
No one's going to judge you.

92
00:04:21,519 --> 00:04:25,124
Okay, for starters,
I think you need

93
00:04:25,148 --> 00:04:27,460
a strong opening
title sequence.

94
00:04:27,484 --> 00:04:28,586
Are you serious?

95
00:04:28,610 --> 00:04:29,795
No one does that anymore.

96
00:04:29,819 --> 00:04:32,739
You just throw up the title
and get on with it.

97
00:05:02,977 --> 00:05:04,663
Walter! Dial 447.

98
00:05:04,687 --> 00:05:09,109
The only way to stop this is to
get the device back to the planet.

99
00:05:22,956 --> 00:05:24,058
Bam, bam!

100
00:05:24,082 --> 00:05:30,630
Look, uh, no offense, but
zombies have been done to death.

101
00:05:31,840 --> 00:05:33,400
No pun intended.

102
00:05:33,424 --> 00:05:36,487
Besides, this is
science fiction, not horror.

103
00:05:36,511 --> 00:05:37,446
Did I mention
the Tel'chak device?

104
00:05:37,470 --> 00:05:40,991
'Cause that's what
turned them into zombies.

105
00:05:41,015 --> 00:05:43,786
Sorry. Go for Marty.

106
00:05:43,810 --> 00:05:46,747
Oh, hey, Charlie. What's up?

107
00:05:46,771 --> 00:05:49,458
It's the studio.

108
00:05:49,482 --> 00:05:50,543
What kind of problem?

109
00:05:50,567 --> 00:05:53,003
Just out of curiosity, what
was the rest of the team doing

110
00:05:53,027 --> 00:05:56,406
while your character was
fighting the zombies?

111
00:06:00,910 --> 00:06:03,806
Son of a bitch!

112
00:06:03,830 --> 00:06:05,015
Studio executives, huh?

113
00:06:05,039 --> 00:06:09,186
What? Oh, no, Charlie,
he's a great guy.

114
00:06:09,210 --> 00:06:11,021
He's the only one I trust.

115
00:06:11,045 --> 00:06:13,232
So, what's the problem then?

116
00:06:13,256 --> 00:06:14,024
Our lead backed out.

117
00:06:14,048 --> 00:06:17,236
How am I supposed to tell a
story without my lead character?

118
00:06:17,260 --> 00:06:20,972
Easy. You just bring in
a character to replace him.

119
00:06:25,810 --> 00:06:27,037
What?

120
00:06:27,061 --> 00:06:28,038
You guys
have to help me.

121
00:06:28,062 --> 00:06:32,042
I mean, how can I keep the
main character in the story

122
00:06:32,066 --> 00:06:35,337
without actually having
the actor who plays him?

123
00:06:35,361 --> 00:06:39,758
Well, you could have the other
characters refer to him all the time,

124
00:06:39,782 --> 00:06:42,052
maybe get him on the phone
once in a while.

125
00:06:42,076 --> 00:06:46,807
Oh, yeah, right.
I mean something cool,

126
00:06:46,831 --> 00:06:51,061
like face-switching
or body-swapping.

127
00:06:51,085 --> 00:06:53,189
As if anyone
would believe that.

128
00:06:53,213 --> 00:06:58,027
Come on, you guys must have some
real-life experiences I can draw on.

129
00:06:58,051 --> 00:07:03,073
Oh, well, there was that time
that Colonel O'Neill was invisible.

130
00:07:03,097 --> 00:07:06,076
So, essentially, what I think
happened is that you were bombarded

131
00:07:06,100 --> 00:07:09,246
by the particle field being
emanated by the cloaking generator.

132
00:07:09,270 --> 00:07:12,082
See, you were in the engine
room standing right next to it

133
00:07:12,106 --> 00:07:13,751
when the device
was turned on.

134
00:07:13,775 --> 00:07:16,337
That would account for
the minute traces of radiation

135
00:07:16,361 --> 00:07:17,087
we've been
picking up from you.

136
00:07:17,111 --> 00:07:20,966
But the good news is, I think I've
found a way to reverse the process.

137
00:07:20,990 --> 00:07:24,929
We have to go back to the mothership
and get the cloaking generator.

138
00:07:24,953 --> 00:07:25,804
Sam, who are you talking to?

139
00:07:25,828 --> 00:07:28,098
Oh, Colonel O'Neill.
I was just explaining to him

140
00:07:28,122 --> 00:07:30,100
how we're going to
make him visible again.

141
00:07:30,124 --> 00:07:32,228
No, you're not.

142
00:07:32,252 --> 00:07:35,105
Jack's in Hammond's office.

143
00:07:35,129 --> 00:07:37,107
I can't believe he did
that to me again. Yeah.

144
00:07:37,131 --> 00:07:40,110
You know, getting that cloaking
generator off that mothership,

145
00:07:40,134 --> 00:07:41,111
that's going to be
the least of our problems.

146
00:07:41,135 --> 00:07:45,407
Now, getting Jack to help,
that's going to be the hard part.

147
00:07:45,431 --> 00:07:47,409
Oh, don't tell me.
Oh, yes.

148
00:07:47,433 --> 00:07:48,893
He likes being invisible.

149
00:07:59,904 --> 00:08:03,008
And so,
by translating this tablet,

150
00:08:03,032 --> 00:08:04,593
we should be
able to determine

151
00:08:04,617 --> 00:08:07,704
the most important thing
in the universe.

152
00:08:16,671 --> 00:08:19,149
Everyone just want
to poke around,

153
00:08:19,173 --> 00:08:20,967
see if you can find him?

154
00:08:22,051 --> 00:08:24,512
Sir, are you there?

155
00:08:28,641 --> 00:08:31,036
Nope.

156
00:08:31,060 --> 00:08:33,789
You cannot remain
this way, O'Neill.

157
00:08:33,813 --> 00:08:34,999
Why not?

158
00:08:35,023 --> 00:08:37,334
It gives us an advantage
over the Goa'uld.

159
00:08:37,358 --> 00:08:41,755
I can sneak around all I want
totally undetected.

160
00:08:41,779 --> 00:08:45,175
I give us
the element of surprise.

161
00:08:45,199 --> 00:08:50,180
The bottom line is I can do
more for this planet invisible

162
00:08:50,204 --> 00:08:53,875
than I ever could as my
own sweet, salient self,

163
00:08:57,086 --> 00:09:00,840
I assume I'm staring
at you stoically.

164
00:09:02,342 --> 00:09:04,069
Not buying it, eh?

165
00:09:04,093 --> 00:09:04,695
No.

166
00:09:04,719 --> 00:09:07,263
You are most transparent,
O'Neill.

167
00:09:08,473 --> 00:09:12,244
Oh, I get it. Good one.

168
00:09:12,268 --> 00:09:14,872
I can see right through you.

169
00:09:14,896 --> 00:09:16,397
Don't push it.

170
00:09:41,214 --> 00:09:44,526
Hello? Hand signals?

171
00:09:44,550 --> 00:09:46,636
I'm waving you over.

172
00:09:48,763 --> 00:09:50,115
Okay, I've made
the necessary adjustments.

173
00:09:50,139 --> 00:09:54,203
All you have to do now is
initiate the sequence.

174
00:09:54,227 --> 00:09:56,121
Okay.

175
00:09:56,145 --> 00:09:57,730
Let's do it.

176
00:10:03,319 --> 00:10:06,882
Oh, yeah.
This is better.

177
00:10:06,906 --> 00:10:10,969
It might feel like
kind of a cheat.

178
00:10:10,993 --> 00:10:14,973
Maybe it's best just not
to mention the guy at all.

179
00:10:14,997 --> 00:10:20,270
Yeah, besides, invisibility
can be very powerful.

180
00:10:20,294 --> 00:10:22,564
You might not want to
open that can of worms.

181
00:10:22,588 --> 00:10:25,275
You don't want your heroes
to become too powerful.

182
00:10:25,299 --> 00:10:29,279
Well, you could always invent
some negative side-effects.

183
00:10:29,303 --> 00:10:32,741
That's how we talked Colonel
O'Neill into becoming visible again.

184
00:10:32,765 --> 00:10:36,245
Okay, that's it, SG-1,
you are cleared to leave.

185
00:10:36,269 --> 00:10:37,704
- Thank you.
- They can't leave!

186
00:10:37,728 --> 00:10:39,289
They haven't finished
reading my script!

187
00:10:39,313 --> 00:10:42,126
That's funny, I thought it
said "General" on my uniform.

188
00:10:42,150 --> 00:10:45,295
We owe you one, sir.
You mean another one.

189
00:10:45,319 --> 00:10:46,255
What are you talking about?

190
00:10:46,279 --> 00:10:47,506
I thought you said
it was going to be fun.

191
00:10:47,530 --> 00:10:50,008
Not after his zombie
idea got shot down.

192
00:10:50,032 --> 00:10:52,553
So, where are you
guys all off to?

193
00:10:52,577 --> 00:10:55,222
Some place pretty important,

194
00:10:55,246 --> 00:10:57,307
It's just a recon mission,
isn't it?

195
00:10:57,331 --> 00:10:58,350
"Just" a recon mission?

196
00:10:58,374 --> 00:10:59,309
This is no simple
recon mission.

197
00:10:59,333 --> 00:11:02,646
This is no less than my
200th trip through the gate.

198
00:11:02,670 --> 00:11:03,397
Really?

199
00:11:03,421 --> 00:11:04,314
You're counting?
You bet.

200
00:11:04,338 --> 00:11:07,526
I have been reflecting
while changing into our gear.

201
00:11:07,550 --> 00:11:11,321
I believe this is far from your
200th mission, Colonel Mitchell.

202
00:11:11,345 --> 00:11:12,698
I didn't say 200th mission.

203
00:11:12,722 --> 00:11:14,783
I said 200th trip
through the gate.

204
00:11:14,807 --> 00:11:16,243
That's counting
off-world gates,

205
00:11:16,267 --> 00:11:17,911
back and forth,
pretty much anytime

206
00:11:17,935 --> 00:11:19,705
I crossed the event horizon.

207
00:11:19,729 --> 00:11:23,333
Yeah.
This is going to be huge.

208
00:11:23,357 --> 00:11:25,568
The big 200.

209
00:11:38,372 --> 00:11:40,350
Fix it.
Quickly.

210
00:11:40,374 --> 00:11:41,768
I'm on it.

211
00:11:41,792 --> 00:11:43,461
Indeed.

212
00:11:47,590 --> 00:11:48,233
Gotta go!

213
00:11:48,257 --> 00:11:51,028
We've got 10 seconds before
the time dilation field is activated.

214
00:11:51,052 --> 00:11:55,306
If we don't make it through the
gate, we'll be stuck here forever!

215
00:11:59,352 --> 00:12:02,289
Okay, this could be a problem.

216
00:12:02,313 --> 00:12:04,374
That was close, huh?

217
00:12:04,398 --> 00:12:05,209
Oh, yeah.

218
00:12:05,233 --> 00:12:07,711
- Are you serious?
- What?

219
00:12:07,735 --> 00:12:09,838
How did we escape?

220
00:12:09,862 --> 00:12:10,464
They.

221
00:12:10,488 --> 00:12:14,426
Fine, they, they, they.
How did they escape?

222
00:12:14,450 --> 00:12:15,385
Isn't it obvious?

223
00:12:15,409 --> 00:12:17,513
Even if the valley
wasn't filled with Jaffa,

224
00:12:17,537 --> 00:12:22,392
we could never have made it to the
gate and dialed out in under 10 seconds.

225
00:12:22,416 --> 00:12:24,811
Good.
See, that's why we're here.

226
00:12:24,835 --> 00:12:27,523
So, what do you think?

227
00:12:27,547 --> 00:12:30,484
Maybe not such
a round number.

228
00:12:30,508 --> 00:12:31,485
How about 38?

229
00:12:31,509 --> 00:12:34,821
What difference does it make? I
mean, it's not like you're going to have

230
00:12:34,845 --> 00:12:37,324
an actual ticking clock
on the screen.

231
00:12:37,348 --> 00:12:38,867
That's brilliant!

232
00:12:38,891 --> 00:12:39,576
That's ridiculous.

233
00:12:39,600 --> 00:12:43,413
Trust me, jeopardy plus
ticking clock is box office.

234
00:12:43,437 --> 00:12:46,959
It's the E equals MC-squared
of the entertainment world.

235
00:12:46,983 --> 00:12:47,709
Ask any executive.

236
00:12:47,733 --> 00:12:51,755
Except I think you've replaced
jeopardy with certain death.

237
00:12:51,779 --> 00:12:53,799
Oh, come on.
You guys have escaped

238
00:12:53,823 --> 00:12:57,010
situations more
dire than this before.

239
00:12:57,034 --> 00:12:58,220
He has a point.

240
00:12:58,244 --> 00:13:00,305
So show the actual escape.

241
00:13:00,329 --> 00:13:01,306
Oh, no, no, no.

242
00:13:01,330 --> 00:13:03,767
You can't give away
too much, too early.

243
00:13:03,791 --> 00:13:04,601
It'll step on the ending.

244
00:13:04,625 --> 00:13:08,105
Maybe if my character
doesn't say, "This is a problem."

245
00:13:08,129 --> 00:13:11,066
He could say something like,
"This should be easy."

246
00:13:11,090 --> 00:13:13,527
How many times
do I have to tell you?

247
00:13:13,551 --> 00:13:14,486
It's not you.

248
00:13:14,510 --> 00:13:18,681
Colonel Danning
is based on Colonel O'Neill.

249
00:13:21,726 --> 00:13:24,454
Please tell me you have
the gate working again.

250
00:13:24,478 --> 00:13:26,206
We're running
another diagnostic,

251
00:13:26,230 --> 00:13:26,915
but right now,
we're stumped.

252
00:13:26,939 --> 00:13:28,709
The power's getting through
to the capacitors,

253
00:13:28,733 --> 00:13:31,420
but, for some reason,
the charge isn't holding.

254
00:13:31,444 --> 00:13:32,838
That's causing
the control crystal

255
00:13:32,862 --> 00:13:34,464
to send feedback
into the interface

256
00:13:34,488 --> 00:13:35,757
and reset
the programing code

257
00:13:35,781 --> 00:13:38,135
of the base computer's
dialing protocol.

258
00:13:38,159 --> 00:13:42,163
Whoa! That was awesome.
Say that again.

259
00:13:43,372 --> 00:13:45,183
No.

260
00:13:45,207 --> 00:13:47,477
Oh. Everybody, take five.

261
00:13:47,501 --> 00:13:49,896
I've got to get that down
before I forget it.

262
00:13:49,920 --> 00:13:52,232
The power getting
to the flux capacitor,

263
00:13:52,256 --> 00:13:56,528
but feedback is not feeding
back into the feedback face.

264
00:13:56,552 --> 00:13:57,863
This is gold!

265
00:13:57,887 --> 00:13:59,448
Hey.

266
00:13:59,472 --> 00:14:00,991
Forget about the techno talk.

267
00:14:01,015 --> 00:14:04,119
No one's really
interested in it.

268
00:14:04,143 --> 00:14:05,162
You're an alien, right?

269
00:14:05,186 --> 00:14:07,664
Exactly, and I know
just what this movie needs.

270
00:14:07,688 --> 00:14:10,751
So how would you know
what sci-fi fans from Earth

271
00:14:10,775 --> 00:14:13,378
would be interested in?

272
00:14:13,402 --> 00:14:15,047
Aren't you also an alien?

273
00:14:15,071 --> 00:14:17,341
Yeah, but I've been here
quite a while.

274
00:14:17,365 --> 00:14:20,677
Right. Look, I think I know
a good story when I hear one.

275
00:14:20,701 --> 00:14:24,640
Don't you want this movie to
appeal to a broad range of people?

276
00:14:24,664 --> 00:14:25,307
From Earth.

277
00:14:25,331 --> 00:14:27,351
I have all kinds of
fabulous adventures,

278
00:14:27,375 --> 00:14:32,397
none of which have been
classified by the Air Force.

279
00:14:32,421 --> 00:14:34,775
Okay, shoot.
Give me your best one.

280
00:14:34,799 --> 00:14:35,400
Right.

281
00:14:35,424 --> 00:14:38,403
I was in a stolen cargo ship,
on my way home,

282
00:14:38,427 --> 00:14:40,322
when a solar flare
from a nearby star

283
00:14:40,346 --> 00:14:42,199
wreaked havoc with
my navigation system

284
00:14:42,223 --> 00:14:45,369
and I was forced to crash-land
on the nearest planet.

285
00:14:45,393 --> 00:14:46,161
In a bizarre twist of fate,

286
00:14:46,185 --> 00:14:48,955
I crashed right on top of the
Goa'uld who ruled that planet,

287
00:14:48,979 --> 00:14:53,210
which was fortunate because my
ship was too badly damaged to repair

288
00:14:53,234 --> 00:14:54,628
and I needed help.

289
00:14:54,652 --> 00:14:58,340
The local villagers
were very grateful.

290
00:14:58,364 --> 00:15:01,677
They introduced me to
a lovely fair-haired Tok'ra

291
00:15:01,701 --> 00:15:03,553
who had been hiding
out on the planet.

292
00:15:03,577 --> 00:15:06,306
She told me the legend
of a powerful Ascended being

293
00:15:06,330 --> 00:15:10,102
who supposedly lived in
some distant, far-off mountains

294
00:15:10,126 --> 00:15:11,269
through a treacherous forest.

295
00:15:11,293 --> 00:15:12,854
Look, I don't have all day.
Cut to the chase.

296
00:15:12,878 --> 00:15:16,024
All right. I met up with a number
of my friends along the way,

297
00:15:16,048 --> 00:15:18,568
and after a dangerous
and eventful journey,

298
00:15:18,592 --> 00:15:22,864
we finally come face to face
with the Ascended being.

299
00:15:22,888 --> 00:15:24,616
What can I do for you?

300
00:15:24,640 --> 00:15:27,119
Well, at first I thought
I just wanted to go home,

301
00:15:27,143 --> 00:15:31,623
but now I've decided I'd quite
like to be a part of something.

302
00:15:31,647 --> 00:15:35,168
A regular part,
if you catch my drift.

303
00:15:35,192 --> 00:15:39,321
Oh, and these guys have
their own issues. Boys?

304
00:15:50,499 --> 00:15:52,501
That's The Wizard of Oz.

305
00:15:53,502 --> 00:15:56,606
Oh, you've seen that one?

306
00:15:56,630 --> 00:15:58,066
Uh, yeah.

307
00:15:58,090 --> 00:16:01,570
Oh, that's my phone.
Martin Lloyd.

308
00:16:01,594 --> 00:16:03,447
I didn't hear it ring.

309
00:16:03,471 --> 00:16:04,906
It's on vibrate.

310
00:16:04,930 --> 00:16:06,908
Yes, go ahead.

311
00:16:06,932 --> 00:16:08,434
I can sing.

312
00:16:11,729 --> 00:16:13,457
So how's it going?

313
00:16:13,481 --> 00:16:14,624
Not well.

314
00:16:14,648 --> 00:16:16,168
Samantha, 200 is waiting.

315
00:16:16,192 --> 00:16:18,253
Right now, I'm just hoping
we haven't done something

316
00:16:18,277 --> 00:16:19,796
to permanently
damage the gate.

317
00:16:19,820 --> 00:16:22,591
How can something work
perfectly fine for 10 years

318
00:16:22,615 --> 00:16:24,468
then all of a sudden
it doesn't work anymore?

319
00:16:24,492 --> 00:16:27,453
I don't know.
Okay, try it now.

320
00:16:29,413 --> 00:16:34,352
Uh, we're getting some strange
power readings here, Colonel.

321
00:16:34,376 --> 00:16:36,605
Shut it down.

322
00:16:36,629 --> 00:16:37,481
It's not responding.

323
00:16:37,505 --> 00:16:40,734
The gate's drawing massive
amounts of energy into its capacitors.

324
00:16:40,758 --> 00:16:43,469
Close the blast door
and the iris.

325
00:16:52,269 --> 00:16:54,331
I can't shut it down.

326
00:16:54,355 --> 00:16:56,607
Siler,
manually cut the power!

327
00:17:07,243 --> 00:17:10,680
Why does this
always happen to me?

328
00:17:10,704 --> 00:17:14,226
Sir, we have to
evacuate the base!

329
00:17:14,250 --> 00:17:15,227
This is General Landry.

330
00:17:15,251 --> 00:17:17,062
I'm declaring
a code red emergency.

331
00:17:17,086 --> 00:17:19,856
All personnel,
evacuate the base immediately.

332
00:17:19,880 --> 00:17:22,067
Sir, the capacitors
are overloading.

333
00:17:22,091 --> 00:17:24,236
We have to get
out of here now.

334
00:17:24,260 --> 00:17:25,469
Hurry.

335
00:17:31,392 --> 00:17:34,979
And that is
the end of act two.

336
00:17:37,940 --> 00:17:39,584
The mountain blows up?

337
00:17:39,608 --> 00:17:40,961
No possible hope
for survival.

338
00:17:40,985 --> 00:17:45,131
Cool, huh? I just wrote it based
on what's going on with the gate.

339
00:17:45,155 --> 00:17:47,759
I love it
when art imitates life.

340
00:17:47,783 --> 00:17:51,221
Hang on. We're alive
in the next scene.

341
00:17:51,245 --> 00:17:53,640
Oh, I just haven't
fixed that part yet.

342
00:17:53,664 --> 00:17:55,851
I'm thinking I can
back-sell it and say

343
00:17:55,875 --> 00:17:58,603
you were beamed out
at the last second.

344
00:17:58,627 --> 00:17:59,813
Beamed out?

345
00:17:59,837 --> 00:18:00,772
Sure. Why not?

346
00:18:00,796 --> 00:18:02,482
Is that not too convenient?

347
00:18:02,506 --> 00:18:04,359
Not if you hang
a lantern on it.

348
00:18:04,383 --> 00:18:06,194
What's that?
It's a writer's term.

349
00:18:06,218 --> 00:18:10,198
Another character points out
how convenient it is.

350
00:18:10,222 --> 00:18:15,203
Dr. Levant can say, "Well,
that was great timing."

351
00:18:15,227 --> 00:18:17,038
That way the audience knows

352
00:18:17,062 --> 00:18:19,958
I intended for it
to be convenient,

353
00:18:19,982 --> 00:18:21,793
and we move on.

354
00:18:21,817 --> 00:18:23,336
Really.

355
00:18:23,360 --> 00:18:25,005
Okay, where were we?

356
00:18:25,029 --> 00:18:26,047
Scene 24.

357
00:18:26,071 --> 00:18:29,718
Oh, great,
one of my favorite scenes.

358
00:18:29,742 --> 00:18:31,285
Go ahead.

359
00:18:34,455 --> 00:18:36,683
Um, "Interior Bridge.

360
00:18:36,707 --> 00:18:38,643
"The crew
mans their stations."

361
00:18:38,667 --> 00:18:40,812
I'm picking up a strange
energy anomaly, Colonel.

362
00:18:40,836 --> 00:18:43,607
It appears the singularity
is about to explode.

363
00:18:43,631 --> 00:18:44,774
Weapons are at maximum.

364
00:18:44,798 --> 00:18:46,693
Damn it, Captain!
Solar flare.

365
00:18:46,717 --> 00:18:48,862
We've got a shockwave
heading straight for us.

366
00:18:48,886 --> 00:18:52,198
Can you reverse the polarity?

367
00:18:52,222 --> 00:18:54,284
I'll do my best.

368
00:18:54,308 --> 00:18:56,786
Engine room. Warp speed!

369
00:18:56,810 --> 00:19:00,498
Take us out of here now.

370
00:19:00,522 --> 00:19:02,125
I'm giving it all
I've got, Captain,

371
00:19:02,149 --> 00:19:03,793
but you're expecting
a bloody miracle.

372
00:19:03,817 --> 00:19:06,796
Hang on, hang on, hang on. What?

373
00:19:06,820 --> 00:19:09,007
Okay, one, that's Star Trek.

374
00:19:09,031 --> 00:19:11,801
And two, it's ridiculous.

375
00:19:11,825 --> 00:19:12,802
What's wrong with it?

376
00:19:12,826 --> 00:19:15,680
The singularity is
about to explode?

377
00:19:15,704 --> 00:19:16,514
Yes.

378
00:19:16,538 --> 00:19:17,891
Everything about
that statement is wrong.

379
00:19:17,915 --> 00:19:21,811
How exactly is having weapons at
maximum going to help the situation?

380
00:19:21,835 --> 00:19:24,814
The audience isn't going
to know the difference.

381
00:19:24,838 --> 00:19:27,943
They love weapons at maximum.

382
00:19:27,967 --> 00:19:29,819
Never underestimate
your audience.

383
00:19:29,843 --> 00:19:31,821
They're generally sensitive,
intelligent people

384
00:19:31,845 --> 00:19:34,824
who respond positively
to quality entertainment.

385
00:19:34,848 --> 00:19:37,744
I do not understand
why everything in this script

386
00:19:37,768 --> 00:19:38,995
must inevitably explode.

387
00:19:39,019 --> 00:19:40,830
Look,
you guys may all know

388
00:19:40,854 --> 00:19:43,583
how things really work
out there in the galaxy,

389
00:19:43,607 --> 00:19:45,502
but I know the film business.

390
00:19:45,526 --> 00:19:48,964
Explosions make
great trailers.

391
00:19:48,988 --> 00:19:50,465
Simple fact.

392
00:19:50,489 --> 00:19:52,842
More explosions,
better trailer.

393
00:19:52,866 --> 00:19:55,577
Better trailer, more viewers.

394
00:19:59,832 --> 00:20:04,938
Yeah, go for Marty.
Oh, hey, Nora.

395
00:20:04,962 --> 00:20:08,692
What? That's ridiculous.

396
00:20:08,716 --> 00:20:10,860
Tell them to stop panicking.

397
00:20:10,884 --> 00:20:13,196
We're going to sign him.
Don't worry.

398
00:20:13,220 --> 00:20:15,323
I already put in a call
to his manager.

399
00:20:15,347 --> 00:20:19,435
We'll get back to you.
Yeah. Bye.

400
00:20:20,769 --> 00:20:22,872
So, trouble with Nora?

401
00:20:22,896 --> 00:20:24,416
No. Nora, she's great.

402
00:20:24,440 --> 00:20:26,876
But since Nick Marlowe
is holding out,

403
00:20:26,900 --> 00:20:30,422
one of the new junior
executives at the network

404
00:20:30,446 --> 00:20:34,050
has suggested we re-cast
the whole movie with younger,

405
00:20:34,074 --> 00:20:36,302
edgier versions of the team.

406
00:20:36,326 --> 00:20:38,037
Can you imagine that?

407
00:20:49,214 --> 00:20:51,067
Yo, dawg,
what did you do that for?

408
00:20:51,091 --> 00:20:53,611
Dude, that hot chick
was so totally a Goa'uld!

409
00:20:53,635 --> 00:20:55,655
Duh, I was still
gonna tap that.

410
00:20:55,679 --> 00:20:57,782
You don't appreciate me,
do you?

411
00:20:57,806 --> 00:21:00,726
I think you're
so totally awesome.

412
00:21:03,312 --> 00:21:05,439
That's what I'm talking about.

413
00:21:07,900 --> 00:21:08,918
Yo, Wonderbread,

414
00:21:08,942 --> 00:21:11,880
you got that address
figured out yet?

415
00:21:11,904 --> 00:21:16,342
Chill, T. I'm, like,
translating as fast as I can.

416
00:21:16,366 --> 00:21:20,305
I'm so sick of being treated like
some sort of object to be worshiped.

417
00:21:20,329 --> 00:21:22,015
I'm a real person
with real feelings.

418
00:21:22,039 --> 00:21:27,812
You know, I don't think
Mitchell likes me anymore.

419
00:21:27,836 --> 00:21:29,379
I'm pregnant.

420
00:21:30,839 --> 00:21:32,817
No.

421
00:21:32,841 --> 00:21:34,093
Nope.

422
00:21:36,845 --> 00:21:39,532
I need a latte.

423
00:21:39,556 --> 00:21:42,118
How about this one? We
were in a cloaked cargo ship

424
00:21:42,142 --> 00:21:44,954
on a simple, three-hour
reconnaissance mission...

425
00:21:44,978 --> 00:21:46,581
Gilligan's Island, right?

426
00:21:46,605 --> 00:21:49,542
You got that from "Three-hour
reconnaissance mission?"

427
00:21:49,566 --> 00:21:50,293
Piece of advice.

428
00:21:50,317 --> 00:21:51,961
If you're going to
rip something off,

429
00:21:51,985 --> 00:21:54,547
think of something
a little more obscure.

430
00:21:54,571 --> 00:21:56,549
Oh, okay.

431
00:21:56,573 --> 00:21:57,050
Call me fahrbot,

432
00:21:57,074 --> 00:21:59,677
but they're gonna have our
mivonks on a platter we don't

433
00:21:59,701 --> 00:22:01,304
starburst the draz
out of here.

434
00:22:01,328 --> 00:22:04,373
The cluster's been damaged.
We're not going anywhere.

435
00:22:06,250 --> 00:22:08,645
Oh, dren.

436
00:22:08,669 --> 00:22:09,920
Hezmanal.

437
00:22:11,964 --> 00:22:13,858
Frell!

438
00:22:13,882 --> 00:22:16,569
Son of a hasmot!

439
00:22:16,593 --> 00:22:17,845
Yotz!

440
00:22:19,888 --> 00:22:21,407
Okay, you got me.

441
00:22:21,431 --> 00:22:22,850
I have no idea what that is.

442
00:22:24,434 --> 00:22:27,646
Oh, for crying
out loud, what now?

443
00:22:28,772 --> 00:22:31,275
Oh, give me a break!

444
00:22:32,734 --> 00:22:36,005
I just a got a text message
from the studio.

445
00:22:36,029 --> 00:22:37,966
The foreign distributor
went bankrupt.

446
00:22:37,990 --> 00:22:39,759
They're slashing my budget.

447
00:22:39,783 --> 00:22:40,969
In a text message?

448
00:22:40,993 --> 00:22:43,680
This totally screws up
the end of act three.

449
00:22:43,704 --> 00:22:45,974
What happens
at the end of act three?

450
00:22:45,998 --> 00:22:48,017
With these cuts, not much.

451
00:22:48,041 --> 00:22:50,043
Act three just ends.

452
00:22:52,212 --> 00:22:53,898
What's so bad about this?

453
00:22:53,922 --> 00:22:56,025
Any idea
when we'll be able to go?

454
00:22:56,049 --> 00:22:56,776
Sorry.

455
00:22:56,800 --> 00:23:00,179
We're trying a full reset
of the system, sir.

456
00:23:01,346 --> 00:23:02,764
Hey.

457
00:23:04,141 --> 00:23:05,827
If you want this
to be more accurate,

458
00:23:05,851 --> 00:23:08,037
why don't you just give him
your mission files?

459
00:23:08,061 --> 00:23:09,038
They're classified.
So?

460
00:23:09,062 --> 00:23:10,665
Didn't you say that
no one on your planet

461
00:23:10,689 --> 00:23:12,041
would believe this
was all real anyway?

462
00:23:12,065 --> 00:23:15,461
Even still, do you know how
many mission files there are?

463
00:23:15,485 --> 00:23:17,922
1,263.

464
00:23:17,946 --> 00:23:20,049
Hopefully 64
by the end of the day.

465
00:23:20,073 --> 00:23:23,094
Actually, it's 1,264 already.

466
00:23:23,118 --> 00:23:24,971
No, it's 63. Pretty sure.

467
00:23:24,995 --> 00:23:26,306
I've read all the files
recently.

468
00:23:26,330 --> 00:23:30,518
Actually,
you haven't read 30185.

469
00:23:30,542 --> 00:23:35,190
30185? What's that?

470
00:23:35,214 --> 00:23:36,816
We can't tell you.

471
00:23:36,840 --> 00:23:38,568
What do you mean
you can't tell me?

472
00:23:38,592 --> 00:23:41,696
I have the highest security
clearance known to mankind.

473
00:23:41,720 --> 00:23:44,157
What is 30185?

474
00:23:44,181 --> 00:23:46,183
We were sworn to secrecy.

475
00:23:47,517 --> 00:23:49,078
Well, why even
mention it to me

476
00:23:49,102 --> 00:23:51,956
if you're not going
to tell me what it is?

477
00:23:51,980 --> 00:23:52,707
Sorry.

478
00:23:52,731 --> 00:23:55,084
Can you tell me?
Oh, yeah, we can tell you.

479
00:23:55,108 --> 00:23:59,339
It has to do with the time that
the gate sent us back to 1969.

480
00:23:59,363 --> 00:24:01,090
Well, that can't have
anything to do with me.

481
00:24:01,114 --> 00:24:02,133
I wasn't born
till a year later.

482
00:24:02,157 --> 00:24:06,304
Actually, it was nine months
before he was born.

483
00:24:06,328 --> 00:24:06,971
What?

484
00:24:06,995 --> 00:24:09,915
You have to remember,
it was the '60s.

485
00:24:10,999 --> 00:24:11,726
Come on,

486
00:24:11,750 --> 00:24:16,940
you have to have known that Jack's
always taken an interest in your life.

487
00:24:16,964 --> 00:24:18,799
Jack O'Neill?

488
00:24:20,008 --> 00:24:21,653
Indeed.

489
00:24:21,677 --> 00:24:23,112
Do you remember
when you were chosen

490
00:24:23,136 --> 00:24:24,030
for the 302 program

491
00:24:24,054 --> 00:24:26,115
even though you didn't think
you should get in?

492
00:24:26,139 --> 00:24:29,077
How about when you were
chosen for SG-1?

493
00:24:29,101 --> 00:24:31,913
Wait, are you saying
that Jack O'Neill is...

494
00:24:31,937 --> 00:24:32,580
My daddy?

495
00:24:32,604 --> 00:24:35,315
All starting to make
sense now, isn't it?

496
00:24:38,568 --> 00:24:40,964
Oh, I'm being punked,
aren't I?

497
00:24:40,988 --> 00:24:45,551
We honestly can't
tell you about 30185.

498
00:24:45,575 --> 00:24:46,719
I can't believe it!

499
00:24:46,743 --> 00:24:49,555
The whole cast heard Marlowe
is holding out for more money,

500
00:24:49,579 --> 00:24:52,058
and now they're
all joining in.

501
00:24:52,082 --> 00:24:54,501
Where do they think
it comes from?

502
00:24:55,752 --> 00:24:58,648
How am I supposed to do
a movie without any actors?

503
00:24:58,672 --> 00:25:00,692
Carter, correct me
if I'm wrong on this,

504
00:25:00,716 --> 00:25:03,278
but is it not a fact of
parallel dimension physics

505
00:25:03,302 --> 00:25:06,072
that each of us exists
somewhere in some universe

506
00:25:06,096 --> 00:25:08,157
in whatever way, shape,
or form we can imagine?

507
00:25:08,181 --> 00:25:10,034
Any way, shape,
or form we can imagine.

508
00:25:10,058 --> 00:25:12,161
We've seen it for ourselves.

509
00:25:12,185 --> 00:25:14,247
There you go.

510
00:25:14,271 --> 00:25:15,981
Use your imagination.

511
00:25:26,074 --> 00:25:27,176
All right, people.

512
00:25:27,200 --> 00:25:30,471
We created this
multi-billion dollar facility

513
00:25:30,495 --> 00:25:31,681
under Cheyenne Mountain

514
00:25:31,705 --> 00:25:34,333
so that we can
use this thing.

515
00:25:35,709 --> 00:25:37,252
Anyone know how?

516
00:25:40,589 --> 00:25:45,069
Uh, sir, we thought you knew.

517
00:25:45,093 --> 00:25:46,362
I do know this,

518
00:25:46,386 --> 00:25:48,573
we need to put
together a team,

519
00:25:48,597 --> 00:25:50,575
starting with
the most beautiful,

520
00:25:50,599 --> 00:25:54,662
battle-ready
scientific genius I know,

521
00:25:54,686 --> 00:25:56,748
Captain Doctor
Samantha Carter.

522
00:25:56,772 --> 00:25:58,499
Reporting for duty, sir.

523
00:25:58,523 --> 00:26:00,776
What can you tell us,
Captain?

524
00:26:02,361 --> 00:26:04,797
Well, at first glance,
I suspect the device creates

525
00:26:04,821 --> 00:26:06,799
a stable wormhole between
superconducting rings

526
00:26:06,823 --> 00:26:09,552
that have been placed in fixed
positions elsewhere in the galaxy...

527
00:26:09,576 --> 00:26:11,220
...converts matter into energy
at the event horizon,

528
00:26:11,244 --> 00:26:13,264
once the initial vortex
has subsided, of course.

529
00:26:13,288 --> 00:26:15,224
And just because my sex
organs are on the inside

530
00:26:15,248 --> 00:26:17,769
instead of the outside doesn't
mean that I can't handle...

531
00:26:17,793 --> 00:26:20,063
Provided, of course,
that sufficient energy

532
00:26:20,087 --> 00:26:20,813
has been channeled
to the device

533
00:26:20,837 --> 00:26:24,049
and that the correct
coordinates have been calculated.

534
00:26:25,258 --> 00:26:27,987
Okay, get to it.

535
00:26:28,011 --> 00:26:30,239
Next, we need
a bookworm adventurer

536
00:26:30,263 --> 00:26:32,241
who can say
"brains" and "guts"

537
00:26:32,265 --> 00:26:33,910
in 27 languages.

538
00:26:33,934 --> 00:26:34,952
Dr. Daniel Jackson.

539
00:26:34,976 --> 00:26:38,063
Why have I been
brought here? This is not...

540
00:26:40,148 --> 00:26:41,459
Ooh, what is that?

541
00:26:41,483 --> 00:26:42,543
The reason
you're here, Doctor.

542
00:26:42,567 --> 00:26:48,490
This big ring was discovered
in the sands of Egypt, near Giza.

543
00:26:49,908 --> 00:26:51,344
Then I was right.

544
00:26:51,368 --> 00:26:53,137
The pyramids really
were landing sites

545
00:26:53,161 --> 00:26:53,930
for interplanetary starships

546
00:26:53,954 --> 00:26:58,142
that enslaved primitive human
populations by posing as their gods.

547
00:26:58,166 --> 00:27:02,021
Well, we found
the ring in the sand.

548
00:27:02,045 --> 00:27:04,190
These symbols,
they look familiar.

549
00:27:04,214 --> 00:27:08,093
They're like constellations.
Don't you see? Here.

550
00:27:10,220 --> 00:27:14,033
If each symbol represents
a specific point in space,

551
00:27:14,057 --> 00:27:17,412
then six of them
would create a sort of box.

552
00:27:17,436 --> 00:27:19,914
The intersection point
between those six points

553
00:27:19,938 --> 00:27:20,957
would indicate a destination.

554
00:27:20,981 --> 00:27:26,421
Maybe, just maybe, the
seventh marks the starting point.

555
00:27:26,445 --> 00:27:29,841
Is there a monitor that shows
these symbols in detail?

556
00:27:29,865 --> 00:27:31,992
Over here, Dr. Jackson.

557
00:27:39,040 --> 00:27:40,101
Wait.

558
00:27:40,125 --> 00:27:42,228
This one seems different.

559
00:27:42,252 --> 00:27:44,021
You found the ring in Egypt.

560
00:27:44,045 --> 00:27:45,314
The pyramid represents Earth.

561
00:27:45,338 --> 00:27:47,650
This symbol has to be
the point of origin.

562
00:27:47,674 --> 00:27:51,237
It was under our noses
the whole time.

563
00:27:51,261 --> 00:27:53,197
I feel so stupid.

564
00:27:53,221 --> 00:27:55,324
And now what this team needs
is a leader,

565
00:27:55,348 --> 00:27:58,911
someone who'll laugh
in the face of his enemy,

566
00:27:58,935 --> 00:28:02,165
even when it's inappropriate.

567
00:28:02,189 --> 00:28:03,833
Colonel Jack O'Neill.

568
00:28:03,857 --> 00:28:06,193
I thought I told you
I retired.

569
00:28:07,694 --> 00:28:10,155
I thought you said
you were tired.

570
00:28:11,239 --> 00:28:14,719
Well, as a matter of fact,
I am a little tired.

571
00:28:14,743 --> 00:28:17,013
There's no time for that now.

572
00:28:17,037 --> 00:28:19,348
You have a mission to lead.

573
00:28:19,372 --> 00:28:20,540
Right!

574
00:28:22,292 --> 00:28:25,271
Sergeant? Make it spin.

575
00:28:25,295 --> 00:28:27,440
Spin?

576
00:28:27,464 --> 00:28:29,442
Sir, it doesn't spin.

577
00:28:29,466 --> 00:28:30,485
What?

578
00:28:30,509 --> 00:28:32,445
It has to spin. It's round.

579
00:28:32,469 --> 00:28:36,115
Spinning is so much cooler
than not spinning.

580
00:28:36,139 --> 00:28:37,366
I'm the General.

581
00:28:37,390 --> 00:28:40,161
I want it to spin. Now!

582
00:28:40,185 --> 00:28:41,478
Yes, sir.

583
00:28:45,148 --> 00:28:47,192
Hey, look at that.

584
00:28:51,279 --> 00:28:53,382
Chevron one is lit up.

585
00:28:53,406 --> 00:28:55,384
I wonder what we're going
to find on the other side.

586
00:28:55,408 --> 00:28:56,844
Whatever it is,
I'll bet it's amazing.

587
00:28:56,868 --> 00:28:59,972
Well, don't get too excited.
It's just a simple recon.

588
00:28:59,996 --> 00:29:02,391
Aren't you the least bit
curious about what's out there?

589
00:29:02,415 --> 00:29:05,978
Well, I'm just hoping we find
some new meat for the team,

590
00:29:06,002 --> 00:29:08,940
preferably something bald,

591
00:29:08,964 --> 00:29:12,026
mysterious, you know,
the warrior type,

592
00:29:12,050 --> 00:29:14,820
with lots of,
you know, muscles.

593
00:29:14,844 --> 00:29:16,864
Sir, the odds against
any alien life forms

594
00:29:16,888 --> 00:29:21,410
we encounter looking remotely
human are astronomical.

595
00:29:21,434 --> 00:29:22,894
Yeah, well...

596
00:29:24,062 --> 00:29:25,230
We'll see.

597
00:29:28,149 --> 00:29:31,194
Chevron seven, also lit up.

598
00:29:39,035 --> 00:29:39,595
Holy...

599
00:29:39,619 --> 00:29:42,431
Colonel O'Neill,
you have a go.

600
00:29:42,455 --> 00:29:43,891
Godspeed.

601
00:29:43,915 --> 00:29:45,875
Easy for him to say.

602
00:29:54,801 --> 00:29:56,320
Okay.

603
00:29:56,344 --> 00:30:02,034
We're all gonna do this
together on three, all right? Okay?

604
00:30:02,058 --> 00:30:03,411
Okay.

605
00:30:03,435 --> 00:30:07,355
One, two, three!

606
00:30:15,322 --> 00:30:17,157
Dear God in heaven.

607
00:30:19,492 --> 00:30:22,287
I feel so stupid.

608
00:30:35,383 --> 00:30:37,945
Yeah, that'll work.

609
00:30:37,969 --> 00:30:40,489
A whole movie
made with puppets.

610
00:30:40,513 --> 00:30:41,365
Hey, I'm just saying...

611
00:30:41,389 --> 00:30:42,491
Maybe we can
have puppet O'Neill

612
00:30:42,515 --> 00:30:47,312
jump over a puppet shark on
a one-third scale motorcycle.

613
00:30:48,438 --> 00:30:49,457
I don't get it.

614
00:30:49,481 --> 00:30:52,251
Look, you don't know
he business like I do.

615
00:30:52,275 --> 00:30:53,586
I don't have any money,

616
00:30:53,610 --> 00:30:58,549
so I have to give
the actors something else.

617
00:30:58,573 --> 00:30:59,634
Bigger trailers?

618
00:30:59,658 --> 00:31:00,509
I can't afford that either.

619
00:31:00,533 --> 00:31:04,513
No, I have to give them
something that costs me nothing

620
00:31:04,537 --> 00:31:07,099
but no actor can refuse.

621
00:31:07,123 --> 00:31:08,100
And what's that?

622
00:31:08,124 --> 00:31:09,644
A good ego-stroking.

623
00:31:09,668 --> 00:31:13,814
I have to make them think hat I
re-wrote the script just for them.

624
00:31:13,838 --> 00:31:15,441
In this draft,
there has to be something

625
00:31:15,465 --> 00:31:18,027
that makes them
want to do this movie

626
00:31:18,051 --> 00:31:19,445
even if it's for scale.

627
00:31:19,469 --> 00:31:22,907
You know,
we've been trying here.

628
00:31:22,931 --> 00:31:23,574
You're not listening.

629
00:31:23,598 --> 00:31:28,329
I'm talking about a twist,
something nobody's expecting.

630
00:31:28,353 --> 00:31:31,147
You mean
something like this?

631
00:31:38,363 --> 00:31:41,550
Wow, I don't think
anyone will see that coming.

632
00:31:41,574 --> 00:31:42,802
No, but there'll be spoilers.

633
00:31:42,826 --> 00:31:46,246
Are you kidding?
It'll be in the commercial.

634
00:31:48,415 --> 00:31:50,351
When it comes to
fighting crime,

635
00:31:50,375 --> 00:31:53,521
there's only one man
keeping the streets safe,

636
00:31:53,545 --> 00:31:55,130
while keeping it real.

637
00:31:59,843 --> 00:32:00,569
Indeed.

638
00:32:00,593 --> 00:32:04,264
Teal'c, P. I.
Coming this fall.

639
00:32:05,306 --> 00:32:06,725
I love it.

640
00:32:08,268 --> 00:32:10,579
I'm just not sure
if the network...

641
00:32:10,603 --> 00:32:13,082
But I'll pitch it
to them next week

642
00:32:13,106 --> 00:32:15,358
and we'll see
what they think.

643
00:32:16,776 --> 00:32:21,841
Oh, I have to get that.
We'll talk later.

644
00:32:21,865 --> 00:32:22,758
You got Marty.

645
00:32:22,782 --> 00:32:25,511
So what brings you
this way, sir?

646
00:32:25,535 --> 00:32:29,807
What, a guy can't just stop
by from Malibu to say hi?

647
00:32:29,831 --> 00:32:32,476
Malibu?
Sure.

648
00:32:32,500 --> 00:32:34,103
I was there on business.

649
00:32:34,127 --> 00:32:36,397
Exactly, sir.
You're an Air Force General

650
00:32:36,421 --> 00:32:38,899
with enormous
responsibilities.

651
00:32:38,923 --> 00:32:40,985
Ostensibly,
I just stopped by to see

652
00:32:41,009 --> 00:32:43,219
how you were doing
with Marty.

653
00:32:45,889 --> 00:32:48,659
Well, truth be known that

654
00:32:48,683 --> 00:32:52,747
lately I've been
feeling a little...

655
00:32:52,771 --> 00:32:56,083
What? How do I say it?

656
00:32:56,107 --> 00:32:58,669
It's okay to say
that you missed us, sir.

657
00:32:58,693 --> 00:32:59,628
No, not that.

658
00:32:59,652 --> 00:33:02,214
Like you have
unfinished business?

659
00:33:02,238 --> 00:33:03,632
Okay.

660
00:33:03,656 --> 00:33:06,844
You need closure,
and I have the perfect thing.

661
00:33:06,868 --> 00:33:09,346
We were about to go on
a mission to commemorate

662
00:33:09,370 --> 00:33:11,515
my 200th trip
through the gate.

663
00:33:11,539 --> 00:33:13,934
Really? 200, you say?

664
00:33:13,958 --> 00:33:17,938
That's 200 times he's
stepped through the gate, sir,

665
00:33:17,962 --> 00:33:19,523
not actual missions.

666
00:33:19,547 --> 00:33:22,651
To be honest, I wouldn't
mind one last jaunt

667
00:33:22,675 --> 00:33:24,469
through the old orifice.

668
00:33:29,557 --> 00:33:31,535
What?

669
00:33:31,559 --> 00:33:34,479
We call it that sometimes,
don't we?

670
00:33:35,480 --> 00:33:37,082
Orifice?

671
00:33:37,106 --> 00:33:39,502
Stupid focus groups.

672
00:33:39,526 --> 00:33:40,669
They hate the ending.

673
00:33:40,693 --> 00:33:42,838
I have to think
of something new.

674
00:33:42,862 --> 00:33:44,757
They could go fishing.

675
00:33:44,781 --> 00:33:45,549
Fishing?

676
00:33:45,573 --> 00:33:47,092
Yeah, it'd be
e perfect ending.

677
00:33:47,116 --> 00:33:52,413
I mean, after that, anything
else would seem pointless.

678
00:33:53,915 --> 00:33:56,227
This is great.

679
00:33:56,251 --> 00:33:57,710
I told you.

680
00:34:02,423 --> 00:34:05,945
Yup, it's good to be here.

681
00:34:05,969 --> 00:34:08,489
You weren't there.

682
00:34:08,513 --> 00:34:12,159
So, what's the twist?
No fish?

683
00:34:12,183 --> 00:34:18,958
No, I need something with
more impact, something more...

684
00:34:18,982 --> 00:34:20,400
Moving.

685
00:34:21,818 --> 00:34:24,153
Everyone loves a wedding.

686
00:34:35,874 --> 00:34:39,711
It's just a summer cold, sir.

687
00:34:41,629 --> 00:34:43,315
A bit of a wait.

688
00:34:43,339 --> 00:34:44,549
Indeed.

689
00:34:48,970 --> 00:34:50,447
You know,
if she doesn't show,

690
00:34:50,471 --> 00:34:53,516
people are gonna think
that you and I are...

691
00:34:55,101 --> 00:34:56,352
What?

692
00:35:13,703 --> 00:35:15,306
Nice entrance.

693
00:35:15,330 --> 00:35:16,932
Sorry, sir.

694
00:35:16,956 --> 00:35:19,268
Carter.

695
00:35:19,292 --> 00:35:21,645
Jack.

696
00:35:21,669 --> 00:35:23,397
Thank you.

697
00:35:23,421 --> 00:35:25,548
Shall we begin?

698
00:35:28,718 --> 00:35:33,782
Yeah, right, if I want to torture
the audience on purpose.

699
00:35:33,806 --> 00:35:37,620
The gate is back up
and running. General.

700
00:35:37,644 --> 00:35:38,787
General.

701
00:35:38,811 --> 00:35:40,605
With permission?

702
00:35:46,486 --> 00:35:49,614
But I still don't
have my ending.

703
00:36:01,084 --> 00:36:04,629
You keep these kids
out of trouble, Jack.

704
00:36:05,713 --> 00:36:07,733
Here's an idea, Hank.

705
00:36:07,757 --> 00:36:10,110
Why don't you
come along with us?

706
00:36:10,134 --> 00:36:11,028
No, it's all right.

707
00:36:11,052 --> 00:36:12,655
No, seriously.
I don't know.

708
00:36:12,679 --> 00:36:13,155
Yeah, come on.

709
00:36:13,179 --> 00:36:17,600
It'll be kind of like
a special occasion deal.

710
00:36:19,268 --> 00:36:20,829
Well, what the hell.
Yeah, that's it.

711
00:36:20,853 --> 00:36:24,917
Hey, Walter!
Come on, we're all going.

712
00:36:24,941 --> 00:36:26,835
I don't have
the right outfit.

713
00:36:26,859 --> 00:36:28,653
You look fine!

714
00:36:44,085 --> 00:36:46,730
Everything ready?

715
00:36:46,754 --> 00:36:49,566
SGs 3 through 18
are waiting, sir.

716
00:36:49,590 --> 00:36:50,651
The cake?

717
00:36:50,675 --> 00:36:52,027
It's all set.

718
00:36:52,051 --> 00:36:54,029
Balloons, streamers,
the works.

719
00:36:54,053 --> 00:36:58,683
All right, let's go check out
the mysteries of P2C-106.

720
00:37:02,228 --> 00:37:03,580
Hey, Marty.

721
00:37:03,604 --> 00:37:04,915
Why don't you come with us?

722
00:37:04,939 --> 00:37:11,088
Maybe find a little inspiration
for the end of your little movie.

723
00:37:11,112 --> 00:37:11,880
I can't.

724
00:37:11,904 --> 00:37:16,593
I just heard from the studio.
The movie's been canceled.

725
00:37:16,617 --> 00:37:18,929
Oh, there's some heartbreak.

726
00:37:18,953 --> 00:37:20,472
Oh, that's too bad.

727
00:37:20,496 --> 00:37:21,473
Not for me.

728
00:37:21,497 --> 00:37:24,584
They decided to renew
the series instead!

729
00:37:28,171 --> 00:37:30,107
Okay.

730
00:37:30,131 --> 00:37:31,758
Let's move out.

731
00:37:46,814 --> 00:37:48,316
And, cut!

732
00:37:51,944 --> 00:37:54,423
Good job, Doug.
Here, here, here.

733
00:37:54,447 --> 00:37:55,924
Ocean.
Thanks.

734
00:37:55,948 --> 00:37:57,051
Well, that wasn't so bad.

735
00:37:57,075 --> 00:37:58,343
And that's a cut, everybody.

736
00:37:58,367 --> 00:38:01,662
That is a wrap on
the 200th episode!

737
00:38:03,498 --> 00:38:05,458
Nice job, everybody.

738
00:38:06,793 --> 00:38:11,607
So, did you ever think a show
like this would go on for 200?

739
00:38:11,631 --> 00:38:12,816
Well, sure.

740
00:38:12,840 --> 00:38:15,176
What do you mean,
"A show like this"?

741
00:38:18,054 --> 00:38:21,015
Martin Lloyd. Yeah.

742
00:38:22,308 --> 00:38:24,870
Are you kidding?

743
00:38:24,894 --> 00:38:28,040
Hey, everybody. Guess what?

744
00:38:28,064 --> 00:38:29,690
The movie's back on!

745
00:38:34,904 --> 00:38:38,092
Wow. Ten seasons,
seven Saturn Awards

746
00:38:38,116 --> 00:38:43,955
for Best Cable or Syndication
Science Fiction Show.

747
00:38:44,956 --> 00:38:46,600
Who would have guessed?

748
00:38:46,624 --> 00:38:49,603
Um, I think, first and
foremost, it has to do,

749
00:38:49,627 --> 00:38:50,979
you know, with the writing.

750
00:38:51,003 --> 00:38:55,984
I mean, obviously we don't
take ourselves too seriously.

751
00:38:56,008 --> 00:39:00,572
Oh, the writers are really
great guys, all of them.

752
00:39:00,596 --> 00:39:02,282
Men, you know.

753
00:39:02,306 --> 00:39:03,992
Yeah,
it's a bit of a boys' club.

754
00:39:04,016 --> 00:39:08,521
The real challenge was coming
up with a good catch phrase.

755
00:39:09,814 --> 00:39:13,335
"It's what I do."

756
00:39:13,359 --> 00:39:17,256
"It's what I do."

757
00:39:17,280 --> 00:39:22,302
That was so, you know, good.

758
00:39:22,326 --> 00:39:24,054
It defined Colonel Danning.

759
00:39:24,078 --> 00:39:27,766
I just don't think they know what
they want to do with my character.

760
00:39:27,790 --> 00:39:33,522
I probably should have
taken up golf a long time ago.

761
00:39:33,546 --> 00:39:35,899
Just... Just kidding.

762
00:39:35,923 --> 00:39:39,027
And I needed something
even better to make

763
00:39:39,051 --> 00:39:43,157
you forget about the guy
that fans loved to watch

764
00:39:43,181 --> 00:39:46,034
for the first seven years
of the show.

765
00:39:46,058 --> 00:39:47,744
I know he was here for eight,

766
00:39:47,768 --> 00:39:49,204
but, you know,
a lot of people say

767
00:39:49,228 --> 00:39:53,041
he just kind of phoned
it in that last season.

768
00:39:53,065 --> 00:39:55,210
Yeah, well,
that's ancient history.

769
00:39:55,234 --> 00:39:57,337
We don't talk
about that around here.

770
00:39:57,361 --> 00:39:58,046
The fact of the matter is,

771
00:39:58,070 --> 00:40:00,007
I don't really even know
what happened there.

772
00:40:00,031 --> 00:40:03,010
You know, to me, it never
felt like I was leaving the show.

773
00:40:03,034 --> 00:40:04,845
I just needed a little space,
a little time, you know?

774
00:40:04,869 --> 00:40:06,722
I wanted to get back to
my roots. Theater, right?

775
00:40:06,746 --> 00:40:10,809
So I go to New York, do a
little Broadway, off-off-Broadway.

776
00:40:10,833 --> 00:40:11,518
Either way,
it doesn't matter.

777
00:40:11,542 --> 00:40:14,563
Acting, you know? Acting
without having people hurling

778
00:40:14,587 --> 00:40:16,940
papier-mache boulders
at you all day.

779
00:40:16,964 --> 00:40:17,733
It was great, you know?

780
00:40:17,757 --> 00:40:20,068
I like them both. I like
boulders. I'm fine with that.

781
00:40:20,092 --> 00:40:23,197
This production runs
like a well-oiled machine,

782
00:40:23,221 --> 00:40:27,075
and, sure, that starts
from the top, but, I mean,

783
00:40:27,099 --> 00:40:29,077
we all love the show.

784
00:40:29,101 --> 00:40:34,291
The gaffers,
the lighting guy, the medic,

785
00:40:34,315 --> 00:40:37,294
the makeup and the caterers.

786
00:40:37,318 --> 00:40:38,128
It's like a family.

787
00:40:38,152 --> 00:40:41,882
The writers were trying to
come up with a catch phrase.

788
00:40:41,906 --> 00:40:47,179
"I can get behind that," was
something we tried for a while.

789
00:40:47,203 --> 00:40:52,935
Anyway, I mean, I'd actually
like to do some writing.

790
00:40:52,959 --> 00:40:53,959
Maybe have a baby.

791
00:40:55,169 --> 00:40:56,921
It just hit me.

792
00:40:58,506 --> 00:40:59,506
Sweet...

793
00:41:03,970 --> 00:41:07,115
And we can get away with
that, too, because it's cable.

794
00:41:07,139 --> 00:41:10,786
You can't get there without the
fans, right? I have to thank them.

795
00:41:10,810 --> 00:41:13,705
I've got to thank
specifically the ones...

796
00:41:13,729 --> 00:41:15,540
Oh, God, what's it called?

797
00:41:15,564 --> 00:41:16,917
SaveDrLevant.com.

798
00:41:16,941 --> 00:41:18,377
Have you seen the site?
I mean, it's insane.

799
00:41:18,401 --> 00:41:21,546
Apparently they get dozens
of hits a month, whatever.

800
00:41:21,570 --> 00:41:22,256
I think that's a lot.

801
00:41:22,280 --> 00:41:25,133
But between them
and the ad in Variety

802
00:41:25,157 --> 00:41:26,510
that they put out,
I'm pretty sure

803
00:41:26,534 --> 00:41:28,303
that went a long way
towards convincing

804
00:41:28,327 --> 00:41:30,013
the producers
to take me back.

805
00:41:30,037 --> 00:41:31,473
Have me... To invite me back.

806
00:41:31,497 --> 00:41:33,141
It wasn't their decision,
you know.

807
00:41:33,165 --> 00:41:34,977
They called, said,
"Hey, do you want it?"

808
00:41:35,001 --> 00:41:35,811
You know, and I was ready.

809
00:41:35,835 --> 00:41:40,232
What I think really makes
this show what it is,

810
00:41:40,256 --> 00:41:45,219
is the chemistry
with the cast.

811
00:41:46,595 --> 00:41:48,532
Yeah. Oh, could you hold on?

812
00:41:48,556 --> 00:41:50,158
Stop the tape.

813
00:41:50,182 --> 00:41:51,660
Who does he think he is?

814
00:41:51,684 --> 00:41:56,873
Well, you tell that ungrateful
little bag to shove it up his.

815
00:41:56,897 --> 00:42:01,986
Yeah, yeah, let him try to
get a job that pays this much.

816
00:42:04,238 --> 00:42:07,634
You weren't rolling, were...
Okay.

817
00:42:07,658 --> 00:42:10,929
Um, what...
What was I saying?

818
00:42:10,953 --> 00:42:13,998
Oh, yes, the cast.

819
00:42:15,249 --> 00:42:16,375
Holy...

820
00:42:22,882 --> 00:42:24,818
That would be, like, a 19.

821
00:42:24,842 --> 00:42:27,195
That's it?
You're good? All right.

822
00:42:27,219 --> 00:42:30,198
Can I get my sandwich,
please?

823
00:42:30,222 --> 00:42:32,200
All right. Hey, thanks, man.

824
00:42:32,224 --> 00:42:34,745
Science fiction is
an existential metaphor

825
00:42:34,769 --> 00:42:39,082
that allows us to tell stories
about the human condition.

826
00:42:39,106 --> 00:42:40,208
Isaac Asimov once said,

827
00:42:40,232 --> 00:42:41,793
"Individual science fiction
stories may seem

828
00:42:41,817 --> 00:42:46,882
"as trivial as ever to the blinder
critics and philosophers of today,

829
00:42:46,906 --> 00:42:48,633
"but the core of
science fiction,

830
00:42:48,657 --> 00:42:50,010
"its essence,
has become crucial

831
00:42:50,034 --> 00:42:53,954
"to our salvation, if we
are to be saved at all."

